@* Section III — Professional Practice *@
<p>
    This is the largest topic on the exam — roughly <strong>30% (27–32 questions)</strong>. It covers how
    professionals actually carry out their work: accountability, duties to employers and clients, standards
    of practice, risk and insurance, the environment, software, stamping, and whistleblowing.
</p>

<h2 id="accountability"><span class="rref">III.1</span> Accountability, Workplace Issues &amp; Standards of Practice</h2>
<p>
    Job responsibility and accountability track the Association’s Salary Survey levels:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Level A</strong> — recent college graduate</li>
    <li><strong>Level B</strong> — Engineer / MIT / EIT / Geol. IT</li>
    <li><strong>Level C</strong> — Professional Engineer</li>
    <li><strong>Level D</strong> — first supervisory or specialist role</li>
    <li><strong>Level E</strong> — middle management or senior specialist</li>
    <li><strong>Level F</strong> — senior management or consultant specialist (<strong>F+</strong> — senior executive)</li>
</ul>
<p>
    A rounded professional combines applied theory, management technique, strong communication, an
    understanding of the social implications of the work, and practical experience. A professional demeanour —
    managing time, double-checking work, communicating clearly, staying positive, and welcoming new
    challenges — is key to maintaining a high standard of practice.
</p>

<h2 id="duties"><span class="rref">III.2</span> Responsibilities to Employers &amp; Clients</h2>
<p>The Code of Ethics defines seven duties:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>To society</strong> — the needs of society always come first</li>
    <li><strong>To employers</strong> — fairness, confidentiality, and loyalty</li>
    <li><strong>To clients</strong> — remain fair, confidential, and loyal</li>
    <li><strong>To colleagues</strong> — politeness and positivity</li>
    <li><strong>To employees &amp; subordinates</strong> — the same courtesy and respect</li>
    <li><strong>To the profession</strong> — preserve its respect and standing</li>
    <li><strong>To oneself</strong> — personal safety and fair compensation</li>
</ul>
<h3>Conflict of interest</h3>
<p>
    A conflict of interest is a situation where competing interests may influence a decision. It isn’t always
    negative or illegal — parties can acknowledge it, give informed consent, and put safeguards in place —
    but it becomes far more serious when it is <strong>undisclosed</strong>. Examples include acting as both
    contractor/supplier and the professional engineer, letting payment sway professional opinions, taking
    undisclosed side work, or charging twice for a service. To mitigate it: eliminate one of the conflicting
    interests, bring in an independent third party, recuse yourself from the vote or discussion, and make
    sure all parties are informed.
</p>

<h2 id="relations"><span class="rref">III.3</span> Relations With Others; Business Practices</h2>
<p>
    Communication and management drive good professional relationships. Treat team members as equals, set
    goals, consult the team for input and stay open to objections, share a proposed solution before deciding,
    and give clear instructions.
</p>

<h2 id="standards"><span class="rref">III.4</span> Statutory &amp; Non-Statutory Standards &amp; Codes of Practice</h2>
<p>Ensuring safety follows a simple procedure:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Comply with design codes and standards</li>
    <li>Be mindful of safety regulations</li>
    <li>Conduct a formal hazard analysis</li>
    <li>Display warning signs where hazards can’t be eliminated</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Standards such as <strong>ASHRAE</strong> and <strong>ISO</strong> are neither federal nor provincial
    codes, yet the courts treat them as the bare minimum acceptable practice. Meeting them keeps your work at
    an acceptable level. Legal standards also protect the public through conditions and warranties and protect
    workers through Occupational Health and Safety — refer to the Standards Council of Canada.
</p>

<h2 id="risk"><span class="rref">III.5</span> Risk Management, Insurance &amp; Due Diligence</h2>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Secondary liability insurance</span>
    <p>
        Beyond primary liability insurance, Engineers Canada administers a secondary program offering
        <strong>$250,000</strong> of worldwide coverage plus unlimited legal defence fees for claims made in
        Canada. It <em>complements</em> the primary plan, which must be exhausted first, and it does
        <strong>not</strong> cover employers, principals, or decision-makers.
    </p>
</div>
<p>Four steps reduce risk:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Remove identified dangers</li>
    <li>Follow approved design guidelines</li>
    <li>Comply with legal requirements</li>
    <li>Adhere to sound engineering principles</li>
</ul>
<p>To analyze risk: discover the risks, eliminate them if you can, safeguard users if you can’t, and warn or fix the issue where protection isn’t feasible.</p>

<h2 id="environment"><span class="rref">III.6</span> Environmental Responsibility &amp; Sustainable Development</h2>
<p>
    The <strong>Exxon Valdez</strong> disaster led to the <strong>CERES</strong> guideline for corporations,
    and <strong>ISO 14001</strong> is the standard for Environmental Management Systems. To fulfil the duty to
    the environment, a licensed professional must follow environmental laws, seek expert help, apply
    professional judgment, fund environmental costs, share information for public safety, and act for
    continual improvement.
</p>

<h2 id="software"><span class="rref">III.7</span> Use of Software; Liability for Software Errors</h2>
<p>
    Licensed professionals are responsible for a program’s results and must understand how it works — but
    cannot guarantee designs or calculations are flawless. Sound practice means knowing the software’s
    capabilities and limits, testing it to validate results, and understanding the engineering principles
    behind it.
</p>

<h2 id="stamping"><span class="rref">III.8</span> Document Authentication &amp; Control</h2>
<p>Applying a seal or stamp is the <strong>last</strong> step of approval. Before stamping, confirm:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Scope</strong> — does the work meet the required scope?</li>
    <li><strong>Accepted practice</strong> — codes, standards, guidelines, and appropriate theory</li>
    <li><strong>Accuracy</strong> — logical correctness and correct transfer of information</li>
    <li><strong>Completeness</strong> — is the work complete and in order?</li>
    <li><strong>Format</strong> — conformity to accepted formatting standards</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The stamp signifies that a licensed member accepts responsibility for the work; it is given only to those
    qualified to practise, and it is <strong>not</strong> a guarantee of accuracy. You may stamp final public
    documents — drawings, reports, geological cross-sections, and final analyses or proposals. You may
    <strong>not</strong> stamp business cards/logos, drafts or “for reference” documents, internal-only
    documents, or contracts and checklists — and a seal may never be used in advertising. The seal must be
    signed and dated. Firms offering services need a <strong>Permit to Practice</strong>, and the Responsible
    Member validates the Professional Work Product with their name, number, signature, licence number, and the date.
</p>

<h2 id="whistleblowing"><span class="rref">III.9</span> Duty to Inform; Whistleblowing</h2>
<p>Facing risky, unethical, or criminal conduct, professionals usually have three options:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Internal correction</strong> — the simplest, most effective, and preferred route; not whistleblowing, because it isn’t public</li>
    <li><strong>Whistleblowing</strong> — a last resort, given the consequences that can follow</li>
    <li><strong>Resigning</strong> — a protest that sends a strong message but does not necessarily protect the public</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Key distinction</span>
    <p>A <strong>troublemaker</strong> leaks information anonymously for personal gain; a <strong>whistleblower</strong> acts to protect the public.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="communication"><span class="rref">III.10</span> Communication</h2>
<p>
    Your Association values effective, respectful, and efficient communication. Common forms include official
    documents, project plans and sketches, simple agreements, and spoken discussions.
</p>
